// expect-error: NITPICK-REACH-003
// expect-error-at: 58:1
//
// THE HEADER THIS FILE DID NOT HAVE UNTIL 0.0.1. See
// `case1_no_failsafe.npk`'s header for why the absence mattered: these three
// files changed behaviour completely on 2026-09-04 and were the only tracked
// `.npk` outside the expect-header sweep that should have been inside it
// (TM-115).
//
// WHAT IT IS NOW: A REGRESSION CASE, and the one that carries the point. O-N11
// landed as the compiler's DEF-5 and this file is refused at `main`, with the
// diagnostic LISTING the arms the absent handler owes -- SIX here, against
// `case1`'s four, and the difference is exactly the import:
//
//     NITPICK-REACH-003 ...: `main` without `failsafe` (D-013): ...
//       -- 6 identities: probe11_arms_lib.EProbeZone, Unreachable, HeapOom,
//          HeapBadRequest, WildLeak, IntOverflow --
//
// So the arm contract is no longer dischargeable by deleting the handler, and
// the diagnostic now says what the handler would have owed -- which is the ask
// this repository made, granted in full. Measured at pin `0dfddac`; no `.ll`
// is written.
//
// ONE CORRECTION TO THE TEXT BELOW, WHICH IS OTHERWISE AS WRITTEN ON
// 2026-09-03: it says the true cause is "here are the NINE arms it owes".
// Nine was never measured -- it is the arm count of
// `../../probe11_failsafe_arms.npk`, a different program. The measured bill
// for this file is six. The sentence is left standing because it is what was
// believed then; this is the correction.
//
// ==================== WHAT THE DEFECT WAS, KEPT AS WRITTEN ==================
// WHY THIS DEFECT MATTERS TO `ntime` RATHER THAN BEING A TIDINESS COMPLAINT.
//
// This file imports `probe11_arms_lib`, which declares `pub error:EProbeZone;`
// and raises it, and CALLS the function that raises it. It has no `failsafe`.
//
// `tests/probe/probe11b_arm_omitted_refused.npk` is the same program WITH a
// `failsafe` that omits the one arm, and it is refused --
// `NITPICK-REACH-002 ... does not name `probe11_arms_lib.EProbeZone``. That
// refusal is the whole mechanism behind TM-017's error budget, `SAFETY.md`
// §2's per-import arm table and TM-013's rule that a fourth identity is a
// MAJOR version.
//
// THIS FILE COMPILES AT EXIT 0 WITH NO DIAGNOSTIC AT ALL. `reach_settle`
// (`src/frontend/analysis/reach.npk`) computes the reachable set, then returns
// early at `if (x.failsafe_decl == 0i32) { pass NIL; }` -- so a program with no
// handler is asked for no arms. The contract is discharged by deleting the
// thing that owes it.
//
// The consumer cannot SHIP the result: `llc` refuses the IR. But the message
// they get is `use of undefined value '@npk_failsafe'` at a generated line, and
// the true cause -- "you have no `failsafe`, and here are the nine arms it
// owes" -- is never printed by anything.
mod:case3_arm_contract_evaded;

use "../../support/probe11_arms_lib.npk".*;

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) {
    int64:utc = zone_offset_strict(0i64) ?| (0i64 - 1i64);
    if (utc != 0i64) { exit 20i32; }
    Result<int64>:bad = zone_offset_strict(9i64);
    if (!bad.is_error) { exit 21i32; }
    exit 0i32;
};
